  "query_preview": "..\nSynoptic Setup: Forecast begins with zonal flow running from off\nthe coast of the Pacific Northwest through the PCNW and Montana.\nNorth of this is cooler air at the surface embedded in a broad \ntrough over southern Canada that has leaked down into the Plains \nand northeast Montana. To the south a large closed low sits over \nthe Four Corners with a ridge just west of it running north from \nthe Baja and cresting over northern California. \n\nToday into tonight: Zonal flow across the region will have weak \nembedded disturbances in the flow that form a modest pressure \ngradient force across the region today. This will lead to breezy \nwest winds in the afternoon which look to be just short of a Lake \nWind Advisory. While it currently doesn't look to be strong enough\nit will need to be monitored for a last minute uptick. In this \nsame vein, downslope wind will generate lower RH across the region\nand if winds were to tick up quite a bit above expected totals \nthen Fire Weather concerns could quickly rear their ugly head as \nwell. Wind speeds and gusts will need to be monitored through the \nday. \n\nWednesday into Friday: As the Four Corners low migrates east onto\nthe Plains, the ridge behind it will widen out along the \nIntermountain West and begin bumping up against the PCNW/MT Zonal\nflow and begin constricting and orienting it to a more NW flow\npattern. The constriction will allow some lift zones to show up\nwhich could lead to little sub-synoptic disturbance for isolated\nchances at showers and thunderstorms as they pass by northeast MT\nduring these times. Though, QPF totals look minimal if anything\nmore than a trace. \n\nFriday night onward: Northwest flow will continue to enhance as\nthe ridge rides up into the PCNW and southern British Columbia. \nThis will make conditions mostly dry into the Weekend. With\nsignificant model breakdown beginning around Sunday/Sunday night \ninto early next work week. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\n- Moderate confidence in winds this afternoon staying below Red \n  Flag or Lake Wind Criteria. \n\n- Low to Moderate confidence in chances for isolated showers\n  Wednesday through Friday.\n\n- Moderate confidence in partial frost Thursday morning.\n\nGAH",
